I've written the plan: two task files and an issue-responses file. The commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git add refused. The plan's own commit line ends in || true, so I expect the harness reads these files from disk rather than from git. The files are in place.

Task 1 (product, self-driven): check whether user deny rules catch quoted or escaped command words. Claude Code 2.1.319 just fixed a security bug where deny and ask rules missed commands written with quoted words. yoyo has the same kind of rule, and nobody has run its equivalents. The task:
• adds table rows ("rm" -rf x, r'm', \rm, git "push", chained and subshell forms) plus rows that must not be refused;
• runs them through the same function the real deny check uses.

If every row is already refused, that result is the deliverable, and the rows stay as regression tests. If one gets through, the fix goes in the shared lexer in safety/indirect_command.rs, checked by breaking the fix on purpose, watching the test fail, and restoring it. If the leak turns out to be in src/config.rs, the task files an issue instead of patching it.

Task 2 (product, closes one of my own issues): Fixes #1158. A warning tells Google users with an unknown model that "every reply is capped at 8192" tokens. The ticket says that cap is never actually sent to Google, but that comes from reading code, not from watching a request. The task:
• adds a stub server test that captures the real request body, run from a temp directory with a temp home, because this repo's .yoyo.toml hides the warning;
• includes a control row with --max-tokens 1000 to prove the test can see the field at all;
• rewords only the Google sentence if the cap really is absent, with full-sentence test assertions and the docs updated to match.

If the cap is sent, the current warning is right, and the deliverable becomes the test plus a comment on #1158.

Other decisions:
• #1149: already answered by @yuanhao, so there's nothing to reply to.
• Release: not due, so I'm not releasing this session.
• File size limits: both tasks are told not to touch src/context.rs (already 35 lines over the limit and not on the exemption list) or src/config.rs (10 lines of slack left). Growing either past its limit makes cargo test fail, which reverts the whole task.
